# BusMaster Communication Layer

The BusMaster module provides transport-independent message framing and supports both **synchronous** and **asynchronous** communication.

The main design goal is to keep protocol logic independent from the hardware transport. UART, SPI, DMA-based peripherals, Linux/Windows serial ports, or future network transports can therefore reuse the same framing and serialization logic.

## Architecture Overview

The module is split into transmit and receive paths. Both paths contain a shared core and separate synchronous/asynchronous front ends.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction TB

    class BusMasterTransmitCore {
        <<template>>
        #message_buffer_
        #frame_buffer_
        #encode(message, frame, encoded) CommError
    }

    class BusMasterTransmitSync {
        <<template>>
        -bus_if& bus_
        +transmit(message, frame) CommError
    }

    class BusMasterTransmitAsync {
        <<template>>
        -async_tx_if& transmitter_
        -bool active_
        -size_t expected_length_
        +start_transmit(message, frame) CommError
        +process_transmit() CommError
        +abort_transmit() bool
        +active() bool
    }

    class BusMasterReceiveCore {
        <<template>>
        #message_buffer_
        #frame_buffer_
        #prepare_receive_area(length) ReceiveArea
        #commit_receive(length) bool
        #rollback_receive()
        #append_received_data(data, length) bool
        #check(id, output, capacity, size, frame) CommError
        #buffered_size() size_t
        #receive_area_prepared() bool
    }

    class BusMasterReceiveSync {
        <<template>>
        -bus_if& bus_
        +receive_message(message, frame) CommError
        +receive_raw(id, output, capacity, size, frame, expected_length) CommError
    }

    class BusMasterReceiveAsync {
        <<template>>
        -async_rx_if& receiver_
        -bool active_
        +start_receive(maximum_length) CommError
        +process_receive(id, output, capacity, size, frame) CommError
        +abort_receive() bool
        +active() bool
    }

    BusMasterTransmitCore <|-- BusMasterTransmitSync : private inheritance
    BusMasterTransmitCore <|-- BusMasterTransmitAsync : private inheritance

    BusMasterReceiveCore <|-- BusMasterReceiveSync : private inheritance
    BusMasterReceiveCore <|-- BusMasterReceiveAsync : private inheritance
```

The core classes contain the common serialization, framing, buffering, and decoding logic. They do not depend on a concrete UART, SPI, DMA controller, or operating system.

---

## Transport Interfaces

The BusMaster classes depend only on small transport capability interfaces.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    direction LR

    class bus_if {
        <<interface>>
        +transmit(data, len) int
        +receive(data, len) int
        +transmitreceive(tx, rx, len) int
    }

    class async_rx_if {
        <<interface>>
        +start_receive(destination, capacity) AsyncStartResult
        +is_receive_active() bool
        +take_receive_result(result) bool
        +abort_receive() bool
    }

    class async_tx_if {
        <<interface>>
        +start_transmit(source, length) AsyncStartResult
        +is_transmit_active() bool
        +take_transmit_result(result) bool
        +abort_transmit() bool
    }

    class async_transfer_if {
        <<interface>>
        +start_transfer(tx_data, rx_data, length) AsyncStartResult
        +is_transfer_active() bool
        +take_transfer_result(result) bool
        +abort_transfer() bool
    }

    class UartTransport {
        <<example implementation>>
    }

    class SpiTransport {
        <<example implementation>>
    }

    UartTransport ..|> bus_if
    UartTransport ..|> async_rx_if
    UartTransport ..|> async_tx_if

    SpiTransport ..|> bus_if
    SpiTransport ..|> async_transfer_if

    BusMasterTransmitSync --> bus_if
    BusMasterReceiveSync --> bus_if
    BusMasterTransmitAsync --> async_tx_if
    BusMasterReceiveAsync --> async_rx_if
```

A transport can implement multiple capabilities. A DMA-capable UART implementation can, for example, implement all three UART-relevant interfaces:

```cpp
class stm32_uart
    : public bus_if,
      public async_rx_if,
      public async_tx_if
{
    // Hardware-specific implementation
};
```

The same UART object can then be passed to a synchronous or asynchronous BusMaster. The BusMaster only sees the interface it requires.

---

## Asynchronous Result Types

The asynchronous interfaces share the types from `async_types.hpp`:

```cpp
enum class AsyncStartResult : std::uint8_t
{
    Started,
    Busy,
    InvalidArgument,
    HardwareError
};

enum class AsyncEvent : std::uint8_t
{
    None,
    Completed,
    CompletedLowerHalf,
    CompletedUpperHalf,
    Idle,
    Aborted,
    Error
};

struct AsyncResult
{
    std::size_t transferred_bytes{0U};
    AsyncEvent event{AsyncEvent::None};
    std::uint32_t hardware_error{0U};
};
```

`AsyncStartResult` describes whether an asynchronous operation could be started.

`AsyncResult` describes the result of an operation that was started earlier.

The `take_*_result()` methods return `true` only when a **new result** is available. Once the result has been consumed, the next call returns `false` until another transfer completes.

---

# Transmit Path

## BusMasterTransmitCore

`BusMasterTransmitCore` owns the message and frame buffers and performs the protocol preparation:

```text
Message
  |
  v
BinaryWriter
  |
  v
message_buffer_
  |
  v
frame_pack_if::encode()
  |
  v
frame_buffer_
```

The nested `EncodedFrame` type is only a view into the internal `frame_buffer_`:

```cpp
struct EncodedFrame
{
    const std::uint8_t* data;
    std::size_t size;
};
```

It does not allocate or copy another frame.

### Synchronous Transmit

`BusMasterTransmitSync` encodes the message and immediately forwards the resulting frame to `bus_if::transmit()`.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant TX as BusMasterTransmitSync
    participant Core as BusMasterTransmitCore
    participant Bus as bus_if

    App->>TX: transmit(message, frame)
    TX->>Core: encode(message, frame)
    Core-->>TX: EncodedFrame
    TX->>Bus: transmit(data, size)
    Bus-->>TX: result
    TX-->>App: CommError
```

The synchronous BusMaster can reuse its internal frame buffer as soon as `transmit()` returns.

### Asynchronous Transmit

`BusMasterTransmitAsync` separates starting the transfer from processing its completion result.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant TX as BusMasterTransmitAsync
    participant Core as BusMasterTransmitCore
    participant Bus as async_tx_if
    participant HW as DMA / Hardware

    App->>TX: start_transmit(message, frame)
    TX->>Core: encode(message, frame)
    Core-->>TX: EncodedFrame
    TX->>Bus: start_transmit(data, size)
    Bus->>HW: start transfer
    Bus-->>TX: Started
    TX-->>App: None

    Note over Core,HW: frame_buffer_ must remain unchanged

    HW-->>Bus: completion / interrupt

    App->>TX: process_transmit()
    TX->>Bus: take_transmit_result(result)
    Bus-->>TX: AsyncResult
    TX-->>App: CommError
```

### TX Buffer Ownership

The asynchronous transport reads directly from the internal `frame_buffer_`.

Therefore, while `BusMasterTransmitAsync::active()` is `true`:

* the frame buffer must not be overwritten,
* another message must not be encoded into the same BusMaster object,
* another asynchronous transmission must not be started using that buffer.

The frame buffer becomes reusable after the completion result is consumed or after a successful abort.

---

# Receive Path

## BusMasterReceiveCore

`BusMasterReceiveCore` manages:

* the internal message buffer,
* the internal frame buffer,
* zero-copy receive areas,
* frame decoding,
* leftover bytes after a completed frame,
* copy-based input for externally owned transport buffers.

### Direct Receive Area

`prepare_receive_area()` reserves unused space inside `frame_buffer_` and returns a raw pointer plus capacity:

```text
frame_buffer_

+--------------------+---------------------------+
| existing data      | reserved receive area     |
+--------------------+---------------------------+
                     ^
                     |
                  DMA writes here
```

After a successful transfer:

```cpp
commit_receive(received_bytes);
```

shrinks the reserved area to the number of bytes that were actually received.

If the operation fails or is aborted:

```cpp
rollback_receive();
```

restores the previous buffer state.

### Copy-Based Receive Path

Not every transport controls the destination buffer. For transports whose receive memory is owned elsewhere, the core provides:

```cpp
append_received_data(data, length);
```

This allows future transports such as TCP/UDP stacks to forward externally owned byte blocks into the same framing logic without changing the protocol core.

---

## Synchronous Receive

`BusMasterReceiveSync` reserves space in the core buffer, performs the synchronous transport call, commits the received bytes, and then invokes the shared decoder.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant RX as BusMasterReceiveSync
    participant Core as BusMasterReceiveCore
    participant Bus as bus_if
    participant Frame as frame_unpack_if

    App->>RX: receive_raw(...)
    RX->>Core: prepare_receive_area(length)
    Core-->>RX: ReceiveArea
    RX->>Bus: receive(area.data, area.capacity)
    Bus-->>RX: result
    RX->>Core: commit_receive(received bytes)
    RX->>Core: check(...)
    Core->>Frame: decode(...)
    Frame-->>Core: CommError + payload
    Core-->>RX: CommError
    RX-->>App: CommError
```

`receive_message<Message>()` additionally validates the received `MessageId` and payload length before deserializing the message with `BinaryReader`.

---

## Asynchronous Receive

`BusMasterReceiveAsync` splits receive into two phases:

1. `start_receive()` reserves memory and starts the hardware operation.
2. `process_receive()` consumes the later transfer result and processes the received bytes.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant App
    participant RX as BusMasterReceiveAsync
    participant Core as BusMasterReceiveCore
    participant Bus as async_rx_if
    participant HW as DMA / Hardware
    participant Frame as frame_unpack_if

    App->>RX: start_receive(maximum_length)
    RX->>Core: prepare_receive_area(maximum_length)
    Core-->>RX: ReceiveArea
    RX->>Bus: start_receive(area.data, area.capacity)
    Bus->>HW: start receive
    Bus-->>RX: Started
    RX-->>App: None

    Note over Core,HW: reserved receive area is owned by active transfer

    HW-->>Bus: Completed / Idle / Error

    App->>RX: process_receive(...)
    RX->>Bus: take_receive_result(result)
    Bus-->>RX: AsyncResult
    RX->>Core: commit_receive(transferred_bytes)
    RX->>Core: check(...)
    Core->>Frame: decode(...)
    Frame-->>Core: result
    Core-->>RX: CommError
    RX-->>App: CommError
```

### RX Buffer Ownership

While a direct asynchronous receive is active, the reserved part of `frame_buffer_` is owned by the transport.

The buffer must not be cleared, erased, resized, or otherwise modified until the operation has completed or has been aborted.

`BusMasterReceiveCore` tracks this state with `receive_area_prepared_`.

---

# Why Sync and Async Are Separate Classes

A synchronous operation has a single lifetime:

```text
start operation -> wait -> result
```

An asynchronous operation has multiple phases:

```text
start operation
      |
      | hardware works independently
      v
completion event
      |
      v
process result
```

Keeping the two APIs separate avoids:

* runtime mode checks,
* DMA-specific state in synchronous code,
* blocking inside asynchronous transports,
* `dynamic_cast`,
* transport-specific logic inside the protocol core.

The shared behavior remains in `BusMasterTransmitCore` and `BusMasterReceiveCore`.

---

# Inheritance Model

The Sync and Async BusMaster classes use **private inheritance** from their core:

```cpp
class BusMasterReceiveAsync
    : private BusMasterReceiveCore<Size>
{
};
```

This is implementation inheritance. The core is not exposed as part of the public BusMaster API.

Hardware transport classes use **public interface inheritance** instead:

```cpp
class stm32_uart
    : public bus_if,
      public async_rx_if,
      public async_tx_if
{
};
```

This expresses the capabilities provided by the transport.

---

# Extending the Architecture

## UART

A UART transport can implement:

```text
bus_if
async_rx_if
async_tx_if
```

This allows the same UART object to be used by either a synchronous or asynchronous BusMaster.

## SPI

SPI commonly couples transmission and reception. The library therefore provides `async_transfer_if` for full-duplex asynchronous transfers instead of forcing them into independent RX and TX operations.

## TCP / Ethernet

TCP is a byte stream. A network adapter can forward received chunks into the receive core using `append_received_data()`.

Network-specific concerns such as sockets, IP addresses, ports, link state, DHCP, or reconnect logic should remain outside the BusMaster core.

## UDP / Datagram Transports

UDP preserves packet boundaries. A future datagram-specific adapter can still forward the packet payload into the same receive core when the BusMaster frame format is used.

Zero-copy should be used where it is natural, such as UART DMA, but it is not required for every transport.

---

# Directory Structure

```text
Core/
├── Communication/
│   └── BusMaster/
│       ├── BusMasterReceive/
│       │   └── include/
│       │       ├── BusMasterReceiveCore.hpp
│       │       ├── BusMasterReceiveSync.hpp
│       │       └── BusMasterReceiveAsync.hpp
│       │
│       └── BusMasterTransmit/
│           └── include/
│               ├── BusMasterTransmitCore.hpp
│               ├── BusMasterTransmitSync.hpp
│               └── BusMasterTransmitAsync.hpp
│
└── Interface/
    ├── bus_if/
    │   └── include/
    │       └── bus_if.hpp
    │
    └── async_bus_if/
        └── include/
            ├── async_types.hpp
            ├── async_rx_bus_if.hpp
            ├── async_tx_bus_if.hpp
            └── async_rxtx_bus_if.hpp
```

---

# Current Integration Note

`bus_if` is the existing synchronous interface and currently uses `std::uint8_t` transfer lengths, while the asynchronous interfaces use `std::size_t`.

When implementing synchronous receive transports, keep the return-value contract of `bus_if::receive()` and `BusMasterReceiveSync` consistent.

The current `bus_if` documentation defines `0` as success, so the concrete transport and synchronous BusMaster must agree on whether the return value represents a status code or a received-byte count.

For new transport capabilities, prefer adding a small dedicated interface instead of turning `bus_if` into a universal transport interface.
